---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '@/components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const revised = '2026-10-15';

const pageTitle = 'APS Research';
const pageDescription =
  'What APS research aims to achieve, what it has established, what remains uncertain, and how its claims are tested.';
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'APS Research' }]} />

    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>APS Research</h1>
      <div class="page-revised">Revised: {revised}</div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        Biology investigates living systems from cells and organisms to populations
        and ecosystems. Different areas of biology use different concepts and
        explanations. Understanding how these perspectives fit together, without
        overlooking their differences, is an important scientific challenge.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Agency–Process–Scale (APS) framework investigates whether important
        biological concepts can be made more precise, their relationships more
        explicit, and their scientific usefulness more readily assessed across
        different areas of biology.
      </p>
      <p>
        APS begins with the idea that living systems maintain the conditions
        necessary for their continued existence through organised biological
        activity. It examines this activity in terms of agency, process and scale,
        and investigates relationships among concepts such as viability, function,
        purpose and cognition. APS is being investigated as a candidate general
        explanatory framework, not presented as an established replacement for
        existing biological theories.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the broader historical and conceptual context, see
        <a href="/articles/aps-as-a-conceptual-framework-for-biology/">APS as a Conceptual Framework for Biology</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>1. What Is APS Research Trying to Achieve?</h2>
      <p>
        The central question is whether APS can help biologists state important
        concepts more clearly, identify how they are connected, and assess those
        connections against biological evidence. Its potential contribution may
        lie in making relationships among existing ideas more systematic and
        usable, rather than in discovering entirely new biological principles.
      </p>
      <p>
        The research distinguishes five questions: Are the concepts scientifically
        precise? Can claims involving them be assessed against evidence? Are their
        relationships stated consistently? Do those relationships prove useful in
        scientific work? And, where a comparative advantage is claimed, does APS
        perform better than the strongest existing alternatives? A positive answer
        to one question does not settle the others.
      </p>

      <h2>2. How Is APS Investigated?</h2>
      <p>
        APS claims are compared with established biological explanations and
        evaluated against relevant evidence. Each comparison identifies the
        specific question being addressed, what the alternatives already explain,
        and what result would count against an APS claim. A clearer description
        is not automatically a better explanation, and an unsuccessful comparison
        is a legitimate research finding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Readers interested in the methods can explore
        <a href="/articles/biological-explanation-what-needs-to-be-explained/">Biological Explanation — What Needs to Be Explained</a>,
        <a href="/articles/how-should-scientific-explanations-be-compared/">How Should Scientific Explanations Be Compared?</a>,
        and <a href="/articles/what-counts-as-explanatory-gain/">What Counts as Explanatory Gain?</a>.
        These explain how comparisons are conducted; they do not themselves
        establish that a particular APS claim succeeds.
      </p>

      <h2>3. What Has the Research Established?</h2>
      <h3>Clearer concepts and relationships</h3>
      <p>
        In an initial assessment of six biological concepts, biological agency,
        function, viability and organised persistence met the specified criteria
        for conceptual precision. Function also met the criteria for empirical
        assessability. The other empirical assessments remain unresolved.
      </p>
      <p>
        A further investigation found qualified support for conceptual
        integration. APS makes explicit relationships between viability and
        agency, function and viability, and viability and organised persistence.
        Relationships involving purpose and cognition still require further
        investigation. This is a bounded result about conceptual organisation,
        not proof of improved scientific practice.
      </p>

      <h3>A coherent framework across biological fields</h3>
      <p>
        APS research has organised its present substantive work into five areas:
        Cognition; Development &amp; Regeneration; Evolutionary Dynamics; Ecology;
        and Social Organisation. These are areas of explanatory work, not five
        separate levels of biological reality. Foundational ideas and methods
        can connect them without becoming additional domains.
      </p>
      <p>
        Subsequent comparisons examined the strongest apparent tensions at the
        boundaries between these areas. No genuine inconsistency was established
        in the cases examined, and the assessment did not warrant adding another
        substantive domain. This supports the coherence of the present
        organisation of APS research. It does not confirm APS across biology or
        show that its explanations are superior to existing ones.
      </p>

      <h3>Methods that can distinguish different outcomes</h3>
      <p>
        Completed methodological investigations have developed ways to compare
        explanations and examine claims about biological dependencies. One
        three-case investigation considered planarian regeneration,
        <em>Pristionchus pacificus</em> mouth-form development and mammalian
        glucose homeostasis. Its differentiated results included a positive
        case, a transfer case and a null case, illustrating the importance of
        recognising when a proposed method does not establish additional
        explanatory gain.
      </p>

      <h2>4. What Remains Uncertain?</h2>
      <p>
        APS has not yet demonstrated that its conceptual integration improves
        scientific practice generally, or that it offers a comparative advantage
        over the strongest existing approaches. Some controlled comparisons have
        found equivalent redescription, local dispensability or no established
        explanatory gain. A study of communication did not demonstrate that APS
        improved reader understanding compared with an alternative presentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        These findings help define the limits of present claims. A coherent
        framework is not the same as an empirically confirmed one; making a
        relationship explicit is not necessarily an explanatory advance; and
        results from one biological setting cannot simply be extended to others.
        Equally, a result described as “not established” is not the same as a
        result shown to be false.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Where Is the Research Going Next?</h2>
      <p>
        Current work is examining whether APS's more explicit conceptual
        relationships can make a demonstrable difference to scientific assessment.
        This requires moving from clarity and coherence to particular biological
        questions, evidence and controlled comparisons.
      </p>
      <p>
        One planned comparison uses experimental research on stomatal regulation
        in <em>Arabidopsis thaliana</em>, assessed alongside established
        organisational theory. Independent verification and comparative
        assessment are not yet complete. The outcome may support, qualify or
        fail to establish an additional APS contribution.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Publications and Research Resources</h2>
      <p>
        The resources below distinguish peer-reviewed publication from completed
        APS research. Completion means that a specified investigation has been
        brought to a conclusion; it does not imply external peer review,
        independent confirmation or a positive result.
      </p>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>Agency as the Defining Activity of Life: A Viability-Oriented Framework Integrating Process and Scale</h3>
        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Peer-reviewed publication</p>
        <p>
          The foundational presentation of APS as a viability-oriented account
          of living organisation, integrating agency, process and scale.
          Spencer, R. D. (2026). <em>Biological Theory</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s13752-026-00547-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOI</a>
          · <a href="https://rdcu.be/fkRpq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SharedIt full-text access</a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3><a href="/articles/comparative-explanatory-methodology-theoretical-biology/">Comparative Explanatory Methodology in Theoretical Biology</a></h3>
        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>
        <p>
          Sets out a common approach to comparing biological research programmes
          while respecting differences in what they aim to explain.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3><a href="/research/auditing-dependency-claims-biological-explanation/">Auditing Dependency Claims in Biological Explanation: A Comparative Method and Three-Case Test</a></h3>
        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>
        <p>
          Tests a method for evaluating claims about biological dependencies
          across three different cases, including a case where additional
          explanatory gain was not established.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>Domain Consolidation and Cross-Domain Reconciliation</h3>
        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>
        <p>
          Establishes the present five-area organisation of APS research and
          examines its strongest apparent boundary tensions. Its conclusions
          concern architectural coherence, not framework-wide validation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <p>
        <a href="/publications/">Publications</a> lists formally released
        scholarly outputs and their publication status.
        The <a href="/articles/">Articles collection</a> offers broader
        explanations of APS concepts and applications.
        <a href="/articles/aps-as-an-evolving-intellectual-system/">APS as an Evolving Intellectual System</a>
        describes how the framework has been developed, revised and assessed.
        The <a href="/policies/citation-policy/">Citation Policy</a>
        explains how to cite APS materials.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>